{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed diagnostic, 'cognitive friction,' fails to causally distinguish a reliability gain from mere compliance: the target identifies friction only by its co-occurrence with the correct outcome (panel notices a checklist/data conflict, resolves it, and is still correct). That same event is consistent with noise or post-hoc rationalization, since any incorrect resolution would be filtered out by the verifiable outcome, leaving survivors that look like genuine reasoning. No causal mechanism is supplied that makes correct conflict-resolution diagnostic of reliability rather than selection bias; the target never shows friction improves expected accuracy over compliance, only that it co-occurs with correctness.","counterexample":[{"checklist_item":"confirm_symptom_X_present","data_observed":"symptom_X ambiguous","panel_action":"overrides checklist, judges case positive","ground_truth":"positive","friction_present":true,"correct":true,"reliability_gain":false}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'decoy check' diagnostic lacks a causal mechanism linking decoy-rejection to reliability for the actual task. Correctly labeling a planted logically-irrelevant item as not-applicable demonstrates competence at the decoy sub-task, not that the panel's checklist-driven judgment on the verifiable outcome is more reliable than an expert's intuition. The target provides no argument that decoy performance causally predicts outcome accuracy or that it separates reliability gain from compliance; it assumes the correlation is diagnostic without modeling the link. A panel could be trained specifically on decoys and still track the right answer via surface compliance.","counterexample":[{"decoy_rejected":true,"outcome_judgment_correct":true,"reliability_gain":false,"explanation":"panel trained to mark planted decoys N/A; outcome still obtained by rote checklist compliance"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'unknown unknowns' argument asserts intuition captures what finite checklists miss, but provides no causal mechanism showing expert intuition is calibrated to those unknowns rather than introducing additional uncontrolled bias. The claim assumes 'compressed expertise' reliably maps to true anomalies, yet never establishes that intuition's hit rate on unknown-unknowns exceeds its false-positive rate in a way that improves expected accuracy on objectively verifiable outcomes. Without that calibration link, the fact that checklists are silent on unknowns does not imply intuition outperforms them; intuition may simply produce untracked errors the checklist would have prevented.","counterexample":[{"domain":"rare_adverse_drug_reaction","expert_intuition_flags":"anomaly","ground_truth":"no_anomaly","expert_false_positive":true,"checklist_would_have_correctly_cleared":true}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"The aggregation argument assumes panel errors are 'largely uncorrelated' because members follow the same checklist, but the shared checklist is itself a common input that induces correlated, not independent, errors. The target offers no causal mechanism establishing why non-experts applying an identical explicit checklist would produce uncorrelated errors; the more natural causal story is shared systematic bias from the common checklist and training. Its own countercondition concedes a systemic checklist error induces shared bias, but the core mechanism never rules out the milder, omnipresent version: the checklist's design causes correlated mistakes even absent a 'systemic error.' Thus aggregation may amplify rather than cancel the dominant error component.","counterexample":[{"shared_checklist_item":"mis-specifies_threshold","panel_members":["all apply same threshold"],"errors":"perfectly correlated across panel","aggregation":"majority vote preserves the shared error","ground_truth":"not matched"}],"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}